id: ops-3wbz
title: "An assignment the scanner cannot read leaves the previous binding standing, so a replaced provenance flag still attests the publish"
description: "Measured against a 30-case bypass corpus run through verify() on a throwaway git repository, the canonical auditor admits two unattested publish shapes.\n\nFLAG=--provenance followed by FLAG=$OTHER, then npm publish $FLAG: the shell passes whatever $OTHER holds, but shellScalarsByLine only ever calls scalars.set for an assignment literalScalarAssignments could read. A non-literal right-hand side is not returned at all, and a literal one carrying a shell metacharacter is skipped by the metacharacter guard, so in both cases the loop never touches the map and the earlier --provenance binding survives the line that replaced it. The same holds for FLAG=$(cat file).\n\nThe defect is one rule applied in the wrong direction: an assignment the scanner cannot read is treated as binding-unchanged when the shell has made it binding-no-longer-known. Every other unknown in this scanner already fails closed - expandScalars leaves an unknown name in place precisely so that not-understood cannot read as carries-no-flags.\n\nCorpus verdicts, canonical implementation: nonliteral-overwrite=pass (expected fail), nonliteral-overwrite-cmdsub=pass (expected fail); the other 28 cases, including both node-script false-failure controls, are correct."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[3]: attestation,fail-open,security
created_at: "2026-09-07T09:40:50.515Z"
updated_at: "2026-09-07T09:54:57.400Z"
closed_at: "2026-09-07T09:50:31.966Z"
completed_at: "2026-09-07T09:50:31.966Z"
claim_principal: claude
author: claude
acceptance_criteria: "A non-literal assignment to a name removes that name's binding, so a later expansion of it cannot supply --provenance; A literal assignment whose value carries a shell metacharacter also removes the binding rather than leaving the previous value standing; Reverting the production change makes the new regression tests fail"
comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-09-07T09:54:57.400Z",claude,"Fixing the reported defect surfaced a third of the same class, unreported until now. FLAG=\"--provenance;\" and FLAG='--provenance;' bind one literal word --provenance; which npm receives as a single unknown argument, so the publish is unattested. The scanner proves attestation by inlining the binding and re-reading the text, and the inlined ; splits the command, leaving a clean npm publish --provenance plus a phantom second command. Confirmed present on origin/main and closed by this change.\n\nThe metacharacter rule that would have caught it already existed, but only in shellScalarsByLine. attestation.ts maintains its own scope-aware binding map and never applied it. The rule now lives in one place, readableValue, on the path both consumers read, which is why one change closes both.\n\nA fourth shape was investigated and REJECTED: FLAG=--provenance\\; appeared to bypass, but the backslash was consumed by the probe's own JS string literal, so the line under test was the ordinary separator FLAG=--provenance; which correctly binds --provenance. Recorded because the artifact was convincing and would otherwise be rediscovered."
close_reason: "Closed three fail-open constructions. Corpus verdict on this repository went from 2 wrong (nonliteral-overwrite, nonliteral-overwrite-cmdsub) to clean across 33 cases, and both reverts of the production change break the new tests: dropping unreadable assignments from the events map fails 9, and making attestation.ts read the literal projection again fails 6. Gates: typecheck pass, docstring 184/184, coverage 100/100/100/100, corpus clean."
body: ""
